📌 Part 2: Reflection on your approach
1. Main principle used to decide query points

The primary strategy was Bayesian optimisation using a Gaussian Process surrogate model, combined with acquisition functions:

Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) for balancing exploration and exploitation in earlier functions
Expected Improvement (EI) for more targeted optimisation in later, higher-dimensional functions

The decision rule was:

Exploit regions with high predicted mean
Explore regions with high uncertainty
Select the next query by maximising the acquisition function

In higher dimensions, I used random candidate sampling to approximate the acquisition maximisation.

2. Most challenging functions and why

The most challenging functions were:

Function 7 (6D) and Function 8 (8D)

Reasons:

High dimensionality makes the search space sparse
GP predictions become less reliable with limited data
Acquisition optimisation becomes approximate
Some dimensions appeared very smooth (large length scales), making it unclear how influential they are

Additional helpful information would include:

Prior knowledge of important hyperparameters
Expected smoothness or noise level of the function
Known parameter sensitivities or bounds
3. Strategy adjustments for future rounds

Based on current observations, I would:

a. Adapt exploration dynamically
Increase exploration (higher beta or xi) if uncertainty remains high
Reduce exploration once promising regions are identified
b. Use learned kernel insights
Focus search on dimensions with small length scales (more sensitive)
Treat large length-scale dimensions as less critical locally
c. Improve candidate selection
Increase number of random candidate points in higher dimensions
Potentially bias sampling toward promising regions
d. Stabilise GP fitting
Keep reasonable bounds on length scales (avoid chasing upper bounds indefinitely)
Tune noise parameter (alpha) based on observed variability
🎯 Final Summary

Across all functions, the approach consistently applied uncertainty-aware optimisation, adapting from structured low-dimensional exploration to approximate high-dimensional search, while using GP kernel behaviour to interpret and guide the optimisation process.